{
 "thread": "tJcDVnBXsfA",
 "subject": "Help converting hierarchical model from JAGS",
 "messages_listed": 5,
 "last_activity": "2012-11-30T20:18:53Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "B0RcmPtgS9wJ",
   "from": "Noah",
   "author": "32bf4f27a31a68db",
   "date": "2012-11-30T17:20:00Z",
   "subject": "Help converting hierarchical model from JAGS",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hello,<br><br>I have a hierarchical model in JAGS.  Appears to work fine, but takes forever on my current machine (latest core, i7, 16GB RAM, etc.)  So, I want to try things in stan.<br><br>Two questions:<br>1) Will it run faster/better from the command line than from within R with the rstan package?<br><br>2) Can someone help me with the format of the model file.  I&#39;m having trouble understanding how to convert this jags code to stan code.  This is a hierarchical model with factors for both &quot;group&quot; and &quot;program&quot;.  Looking at counts of students based on their group membership. (Pre-defined cluster) and the program they might enroll<br><br>Thanks!<br><br>Model below<br>================================<br><br>model{<br><br>    for(i in 1:N){<br>        eta[i] &lt;-  ranef_group[group[i]] + ranef_prog[program[i]]+ b_var_1 * var_1[i] + b_var_2 * var_2[i]  + b_var_3 * var_3[i] <br>        log(lambda[i]) &lt;- eta[i]<br>        count[i] ~ dpois(lambda[i])<br>    }<br>        <br>    b_var_1    ~ dnorm(0, tau_var_1)<br>    b_var_2 ~ dnorm(0, tau_var_2)<br>    b_var_3 ~ dnorm(0, tau_var_3)<br>    <br>    tau_var_1 &lt;- pow(sigma_var_1, -2)<br>    tau_var_2 &lt;- pow(sigma_var_2, -2)<br>    tau_var_3 &lt;- pow(sigma_var_3, -2)<br>    tau_group &lt;- pow(sigma_group, -2)<br>    tau_prog &lt;- pow(sigma_prog, -2)<br>    <br>    sigma_var_1 ~ dunif(0,100)<br>    sigma_var_2 ~ dunif(0,100)<br>    sigma_var_3 ~ dunif(0,100)    <br>    sigma_group ~ dunif(0,100)<br>    sigma_prog ~ dunif(0,100)<br>    <br>    <br>    for (i in 1:n_group){  <br>        ranef_group[i] ~ dnorm(0, tau_group)<br>    }<br>    <br>    for (i in 1:n_prog){  <br>        ranef_prog[i] ~ dnorm(0, tau_prog)<br>    }        <br>}<br><br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "UXQz0NzCZt8J",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2012-11-30T19:35:44Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Help converting hierarchical model from JAGS",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 11/30/12 12:20 PM, Noah wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hello,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I have a hierarchical model in JAGS.  Appears to work fine, but takes forever on my current machine (latest core, i7,\r<br>&gt; 16GB RAM, etc.)  So, I want to try things in stan.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Two questions:\r<br>&gt; 1) Will it run faster/better from the command line than from within R with the rstan package?\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "It runs the same compiled code.  There&#39;s a bit of\r<br>overhead from using R, but that&#39;s only in getting\r<br>data in and out.  It can take more size to store the\r<br>data in R, too.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; 2) Can someone help me with the format of the model file.  I&#39;m having trouble understanding how to convert this jags\r<br>&gt; code to stan code.  This is a hierarchical model with factors for both &quot;group&quot; and &quot;program&quot;.  Looking at counts of\r<br>&gt; students based on their group membership. (Pre-defined cluster) and the program they might enroll\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Thanks!\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Model below\r<br>&gt; ================================\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "You need to declare data and parameters.  Look in\r<br>the manual to see how to do that, and make sure\r<br>you get the constraints on the deviation parameters sigma.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; model{\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;      for(i in 1:N){\r<br>&gt;          eta[i] &lt;-  ranef_group[group[i]] + ranef_prog[program[i]]+ b_var_1 * var_1[i] + b_var_2 * var_2[i]  + b_var_3 *\r<br>&gt; var_3[i]\r<br>&gt;          log(lambda[i]) &lt;- eta[i]\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "No reason to make this separate, you just do\r<br>\r<br>    lambda[i] &lt;- exp(eta[i]);\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;          count[i] ~ dpois(lambda[i])\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "You can just do this in Stan:\r<br>\r<br>   count[i] ~ poisson(exp(lambda[i]));\r<br>\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;      b_var_1    ~ dnorm(0, tau_var_1)\r<br>\r<br>     b_var_1 ~ normal(0,sigma_var_1);\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;      b_var_2 ~ dnorm(0, tau_var_2)\r<br>&gt;      b_var_3 ~ dnorm(0, tau_var_3)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;      tau_var_1 &lt;- pow(sigma_var_1, -2)\r<br>&gt;      tau_var_2 &lt;- pow(sigma_var_2, -2)\r<br>&gt;      tau_var_3 &lt;- pow(sigma_var_3, -2)\r<br>&gt;      tau_group &lt;- pow(sigma_group, -2)\r<br>&gt;      tau_prog &lt;- pow(sigma_prog, -2)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;      sigma_var_1 ~ dunif(0,100)\r<br>&gt;      sigma_var_2 ~ dunif(0,100)\r<br>&gt;      sigma_var_3 ~ dunif(0,100)\r<br>&gt;      sigma_group ~ dunif(0,100)\r<br>&gt;      sigma_prog ~ dunif(0,100)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;      for (i in 1:n_group){\r<br>&gt;          ranef_group[i] ~ dnorm(0, tau_group)\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;      for (i in 1:n_prog){\r<br>&gt;          ranef_prog[i] ~ dnorm(0, tau_prog)\r<br>&gt;      }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Also, things get executed in order, so that if\r<br>you did want to transform a variable for using it with &lt;-\r<br>then you need to do it BEFORE using it.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "5uQv1Vb28UQJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2012-11-30T19:55:22Z",
   "subject": "Re: Help converting hierarchical model from JAGS",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I said this part wrong:<br><br>On Friday, November 30, 2012 2:42:47 PM UTC-5, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><br><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span></div></code></div></div></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  ranef_group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ranef_prog</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">program</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span></div></code></div></div></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>               b_var_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var_1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b_var_2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var_2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b_var_3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var_3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div></div></blockquote><div> <br>The way I set it up, there is no b_var_k but a vector b of length K because doing matrix algebra is faster. So, I should have said<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  eta </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">var</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><code><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">ranef_group</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span></span><span style=\"color:#008\"><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\"> ranef_prog</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">program</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span></span><span style=\"color:#660\"><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>and so on.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ZRjoQ2clRQ0J",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2012-11-30T19:57:18Z",
   "subject": "Re: Help converting hierarchical model from JAGS",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On Friday, November 30, 2012 12:20:00 PM UTC-5, Noah wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">I\r\n have a hierarchical model in JAGS.  Appears to work fine, but takes \r\nforever on my current machine (latest core, i7, 16GB RAM, etc.)  So, I \r\nwant to try things in stan.<br></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><br>For general remarks about speed, including the Matt trick used below, see the FAQ thread:<br><br><a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/4gv3fNCqSNk/J6ZItL2ZJ-IJ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/4gv3fNCqSNk/J6ZItL2ZJ-IJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792468665004000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2V7TrJpfhZFuJYrpp4B88K\">https://groups.google.com/d/msg/stan-users/4gv3fNCqSNk/J6ZItL2ZJ-IJ</a> <br><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Two questions:<br>1) Will it run faster/better from the command line than from within R with the rstan package?<br></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><br>The\r\n speed for 1 chain is essentially the same, but RStan makes it easier to\r\n execute chains in parallel and combine them for analysis. See <br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">help</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sflist2stanfit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br>in rstan for an example of the parallel syntax, assuming you are not using Windows.<br> </div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">2)\r\n Can someone help me with the format of the model file.  I&#39;m having \r\ntrouble understanding how to convert this jags code to stan code.  This \r\nis a hierarchical model with factors for both &quot;group&quot; and &quot;program&quot;.  \r\nLooking at counts of students based on their group membership. \r\n(Pre-defined cluster) and the program they might enroll<br></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> // observations<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> // groups<br>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; P; // programs<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> // covariates<br><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">J</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  int&lt;lower=1&gt; program[P];<br>  matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">var</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> count</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>transformed data {<br>  real zeros[K];<br>  for (k in 1:K) { // needed for normal priors<br>    zeros[k] &lt;- 0.0;<br>  }<br>}<br>parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">                          // coefficients<br></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><code><span>  vector[J]</span><span> ranef_group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">_z;</span><span>              // errors in random effects for groups<br>  vector[P]</span><span> ranef_prog</span><span style=\"color:#660\">_z;</span><span>               // errors in random effects for programs<br></span><span></span></code>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">upper</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">K</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma_b</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// can use something besides 100 for the upper-bound</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">upper</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma_group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// or no upper-bound at all</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ...<br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">upper</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma_prog</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   // but then you probably need a proper prior on sigma_group and sigma_prog<br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>transformed parameters {<br>  vector[J] ranef_group;<br>  vector[P] ranef_prog;<br>  // we call this the Matt trick, see the FAQ thread<br>  ranef_group &lt;- sigma_prog * ranef_group_z;<br>  ranef_prog &lt;- sigma_prog * ranef_prog_z;<br>}<br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span></div></code></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    eta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  ranef_group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ranef_prog</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">program</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span></div></code></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">               // this part will lead to parser errors; see next message for a correction<br>               b_var_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var_1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b_var_2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var_2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b_var_3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> var_3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    count[i] ~ poisson(exp(eta[i]));<br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  // In Stan &gt;= v1.0.4, you will be able to do this, instead of the line directly above:<br>  // count ~ poisson_log(eta);<br><br>  // priors<br>  b ~ normal(zeros, sigma_var);<br>  ranef_group_z ~ normal(0.0, 1.0);<br>  ranef_prog_z ~ normal(0.0, 1.0);<br>  // if you use uniform priors on standard deviations then you don&#39;t need to specify them explicitly<br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>generated quantities {<br>  vector[K] tau_b;<br>  real tau_group;<br>  real tau_prog;<br>  for (k in 1:K) {<br>    tau_b[k] &lt;- 1.0 / pow(sigma_b[k], 2.0);<br>  }<br>  tau_group &lt;- 1.0 / pow(sigma_group, 2.0);<br>  tau_prog &lt;- 1.0 / pow(sigma_prog, 2.0);<br>}<br></span></div></code></div><br>That\r\n is not exactly the same model because putting a uniform prior on a \r\nstandard deviation is not equivalent to putting a uniform prior on a \r\nprecision. But that is basically how it could be done in Stan. The \r\nmanual has a lot of similar code snippets.<br><br>Ben<br></div><div><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
